  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "$\\,$ $5$ is integer or $5$ is positive.\n\nWhat is the negation of the compound statement above?",
  "worked_solution": "De Morgan's law for disjunctions states that the negation of a disjunction of two statements is equivalent to the conjunction of the negations of the individual statements. It can be stated symbolically as follows:\n\n$$\n\u00ac(A\u2228B)=\u00acA\u2227\u00acB\n$$\n\nSo, the negation of our statement is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u00ac(5\\text{\u00a0is integer}\\text{\u00a0or\u00a0}5\\text{\u00a0is positive}) & \u2261\u00ac((5\\text{\u00a0is integer})\u2228(5\\text{\u00a0is positive})) \\\\  & \u2261\u00ac(5\\text{\u00a0is integer})\u2227\u00ac(5\\text{\u00a0is positive}) \\\\  & \u2261(5\\text{\u00a0is not integer})\u2227(5\\text{\u00a0is not positive}) \\\\  & \u22615\\text{\u00a0is not integer}\\text{\u00a0and\u00a0}5\\text{\u00a0is not positive}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
